0,Oto-Manguean languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oto-Manguean_languages,"All of the Oto-Manguean languages that are now spoken are indigenous to Mexico, but the Manguean branch of the family, which is now extinct, was spoken as far south as Nicaragua and Costa Rica.","Chatino, Eastern Highland",Otomanguean
1,"Chatino, Eastern Highland Language... | Ethnologue Free",https://www.ethnologue.com/language/cly/,Eastern Highland Chatino is a stable indigenous language of Mexico. It belongs to the Otomanguean language family.,"Chatino, Eastern Highland",Otomanguean
2,Glottolog 5.3 - Otomanguean,https://glottolog.org/resource/languoid/id/otom1299,Eastern Otomanguean (145). Amuzgo-Mixtecan (61). Eastern Highland Chatino.,"Chatino, Eastern Highland",Otomanguean
3,"Chatino, Eastern Highland",https://docs.verbix.com/Languages/ChatinoEasternHighland,"Language family: Otomanguean, Eastern Otomanguean, Popoloca-Zapotecan, Zapotecan, Chatino, Core Chatino, Coastal Chatino, Eastern Chatino. Number of speakers: 1800. Script: Latin script.","Chatino, Eastern Highland",Otomanguean
4,Tones in San Juan Quiahije Chatino,https://bpb-us-w2.wpmucdn.com/campuspress.yale.edu/dist/4/1207/files/2015/10/Cruz_SSMCA-12wxw2k.pdf,"Chatino forms a language subgroup that is coordinate with the Zapotecan language family within the Otomanguean linguistic stock (Kaufman 1993, Upson & Longacre 1965). There are three varieties of Chatino: Zenzontepec, Tataltepec and Eastern Chatino (Campbell 2013).","Chatino, Eastern Highland",Otomanguean
5,Western Highland Chatino - ctp (Otomanguean) - PeopleGroups.org,https://peoplegroups.org/language/ctp/,"Western Highland Chatino (ctp) is part of the Otomanguean family and Otomanguean, Eastern Otomanguean, Popolocan-Zapotecan, Zapotecan, Chatino class.","Chatino, Eastern Highland",Otomanguean
6,"Phonology, tone and the functions of tone in San Juan Quiahije Chatino",https://www.academia.edu/118170624/Phonology_tone_and_the_functions_of_tone_in_San_Juan_Quiahije_Chatino,"Chatino is one member of this Otomanguean language family (see Figure 1.3), and it belongs to a genetic sub-group within the Zapotecan branch (Upson and Longacre 1965).","Chatino, Eastern Highland",Otomanguean
7,Otomanguean Stock,https://www.meta-religion.com/Linguistics/Languages_of_Mexico/otomanguean_stock.htm,"Chatino has seven important variants, all spoken in Oaxaca. Zapotec is a large subfamily, (possibly with as many as forty mutually unintelligible variants), in the states of Oaxaca and Veracruz.","Chatino, Eastern Highland",Otomanguean
8,"Documentation of Chatino, an Otomanguean... | Culture in Crisis",https://cultureincrisis.org/projects/documentation-of-chatino-an-otomanguean-language-group-of-oaxaca-mexico,"Chatino is a group of closely related language varieties belonging to the Zapotecan branch of the Otomanguean language family. It is spoken by most of the 29,000 Chatinos of Oaxaca, Mexico's, Sierra Madre: universally in some communities but only by elders in others.","Chatino, Eastern Highland",Otomanguean
9,Teotepec Chatino Collection of Justin McIntosh | The Archive of the...,https://www.ailla.utexas.org/islandora/object/ailla:124517,"Teotepec Eastern Chatino is a highly endangered Otomanguean language of Oaxaca, Mexico. A strong emphasis of this project has been placed on speaker training and community participation at historical and cultural documentation.","Chatino, Eastern Highland",Otomanguean
